 slug:'jain', path:'/jain-marriage-biodata', updatedAt:'2026-10-08', lang:'en', name:'Jain', nativeName:'Jain', title:'Jain Marriage Biodata Format | EasyBiodataMaker', description:'Create a Jain marriage biodata with optional sect, family and lineage fields, practical guidance and privacy-first sharing.', template:'sapphire-silver',
 intro:'Jain families can have different matrimonial traditions, so a good biodata should let the family choose the details that matter instead of assuming one checklist fits everyone. Keep religious or community fields precise and optional.',
 sections:[
 {heading:'Use the family’s own Jain terminology', paragraphs:['Jain matrimonial introductions may include information about the family’s tradition or sect, but practices differ between households. If a family identifies with Shvetambara, Digambara or a particular sub-tradition, use the exact wording the family uses. Do not infer a sect from a surname or region.','Food preferences, religious practice and family expectations can be included when relevant, but they should be presented as personal or family preferences rather than universal Jain requirements.']},
 {heading:'Keep lineage and family details verified', paragraphs:['Gotra or family lineage information may be requested by some families. If you include it, verify the spelling and source before sharing. A modern biodata can place these fields in an optional section so the main profile remains focused on education, work and personality.','For career details, list qualification, occupation, designation and location clearly. Business owners can describe their sector without publishing confidential accounts or asset details.']},
 {heading:'Privacy and respectful sharing', paragraphs:['A Jain biodata may circulate within a close community network. Keep the first version conservative: no identity numbers, exact house address, passport details or financial documents. A city and family contact are generally enough.','Do not make health, diet or religious-practice assumptions based on community identity. If something matters to the person, let them describe it themselves in the about or expectations section.']},
 {heading:'Final checklist before sharing', paragraphs:['Before publishing a Jain biodata, verify any sect or tradition wording with the family. Jain matrimonial terminology can vary by household, region and tradition, so it is safer to state a person’s actual identity than to infer one from surname or geography.', 'Avoid treating food preference or religious practice as a universal community rule. If vegetarian or other dietary preferences matter to the individual, write them as personal information. The same principle applies to religious practice: describe what the person actually follows.', 'For privacy, keep the first document focused on compatibility and family introduction. Government IDs, financial statements, exact addresses and passport information should not be part of a biodata circulated through a community network. A clear profile photo, short about section and practical career details usually make the document more useful than a long list of traditional fields.', 'If the family uses additional religious or matching documents, keep them separate from the first biodata. This lets the person decide who receives detailed information instead of sending everything in one file. For Jain families, also check that any sect or tradition wording was confirmed by the person rather than inferred. If diet is important, state the actual personal preference. The document should support a respectful conversation, not decide compatibility through assumptions about the wider community.']},
 ],
